---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ImagePlaceholder from "../../components/ImagePlaceholder.astro";
import AstroNav from "../../components/AstroNav.astro";
import { ASTRO_PAGES, BASE, NT, WF, astroHref } from "../../data/astro-page";
import "../../styles/astro-pages.css";
const idx = ASTRO_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.slug === "imaging-night");
const next = ASTRO_PAGES[idx + 1];
---

<Layout
  title="An Imaging Night | Astrophotography | Henry Nickless"
  description="One night of optical debugging on a 200 mm f/4 Newtonian: sensor tilt, a guide-prism shadow, autofocus backlash and secondary-mirror flares."
  image="veil-nebula.jpg"
  variant="gallery"
>
  <AstroNav current="imaging-night" />

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 0.5rem; padding-bottom: 1rem;">
    <span class="eyebrow">// Astrophotography</span>
    <h1>An Imaging Night</h1>
    <p class="lede">One night of optical debugging on the CarbonStar 200, from planning to root cause.</p>
  </section>


  <p>
    Most nights are uneventful: the sequence runs and I check in from
    inside. This one wasn't, and it shows the whole workflow under
    pressure. Images from the CarbonStar 200 kept coming out with bad
    stars, and I was sure it was coma; I had just bought a new coma
    corrector to fix it. Over one night (October 1&ndash;2, 2026) I tested
    the telescope piece by piece, with Claude analyzing frames, logs and
    screenshots as they came in. There were five separate problems, and
    none of them was coma: a tilted sensor, the guide prism shadowing one
    edge of the frame, autofocus backlash, a refocus trigger too loose
    for f/4, and a known manufacturing flaw on the secondary mirror.
    Four were fixed before midnight.
  </p>

  <div class="night-stats">
    <div><span class="mono">Corner focus spread</span><strong>64 → 12 µm</strong></div>
    <div><span class="mono">Star elongation</span><strong>0.59 → 0.40</strong></div>
    <div><span class="mono">Autofocus fit</span><strong>R² 0.88 → 1.00</strong></div>
    <div><span class="mono">Guiding</span><strong>0.52″ RMS</strong></div>
  </div>

  <ol class="night-log">
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">18:36</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Planning</h3>
        <p>
          The target was the Pleiades: its seven bright stars make a
          collimation check, and the faint stars in the corners would show
          whether the new corrector fixed the "coma." A 73% Moon ruled out
          the faint dust, so the night became a test night, shot through
          the UV/IR-cut filter in 60&nbsp;s subs. With sky glow swamping
          the camera's read noise 50&ndash;100× at f/4, longer subs would
          add almost nothing but risk.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">18:57</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Rebuilding the sequence</h3>
        <p>
          A review of my old NINA sequence turned up guiding left running
          through flats, autofocus in the wrong filter, and a 5&nbsp;°C
          refocus trigger far too loose for an f/4 critical focus zone of
          ±21&nbsp;µm. The rebuilt test sequence loaded but stopped
          instantly with no error; the NINA log showed a missing dither
          trigger crashing validation silently.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">20:14</span>
      <div>
        <h3>First test frames</h3>
        <p>
          A star-shape map of the first frame showed one edge of the field
          softer than the other, with elongation fanning around a point off
          the frame. Coma from wrong spacing would center on the middle of
          the frame; an off-center pattern meant the sensor was tilted.
          Defocused donuts showed collimation was fine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">20:52</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Spacing sweep</h3>
        <p>
          Driving all three tilt-adjuster motors together changes the
          corrector-to-sensor spacing remotely. A short sequence at
          0.2, 0.7 and 1.2&nbsp;mm (autofocus, donuts, guided frames at each)
          picked 0.7&nbsp;mm on every star-size measure.
        </p>
        <figure class="wf-fig wf-plot"><img src={`${NT}spacing-donut-profiles.jpg`} alt="Inside versus outside focus donut profiles at three spacing settings" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Inside vs. outside focus profiles across the spacing sweep</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">21:23</span>
      <div class="night-tilt">
        <h3>Measuring and correcting tilt</h3>

  <p>
    Every optical system has one flat plane where the image actually
    comes to focus. If the sensor isn't perfectly perpendicular to the
    incoming light cone, even by a fraction of a degree, one side of
    the frame sits slightly closer to that plane than the other, and
    stars stretch into small ellipses toward whichever corner is
    furthest from true focus. On a fast, wide-aperture system the depth
    of focus is shallow enough that a tilt error worth caring about is
    measured in thousandths of a millimeter at the sensor's edge, not
    something that's reliably adjustable by hand, especially not
    repeatably across setups. An automated tilt adjuster (fine
    adjustment screws driven under motor control instead of by hand)
    corrects that error in small, repeatable steps and lets the result
    get verified against real star data instead of guesswork, and
    re-checked whenever the imaging train gets reconfigured.
  </p>

  <div class="tilt-figure-row">
    <figure class="tilt-figure">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 460 165" role="img" aria-label="Diagram comparing star shapes across the sensor with tilt present versus corrected">
        <g transform="translate(10,15)">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="180" height="110" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-500)" stroke-width="1.5" />
          <circle cx="14" cy="14" r="2.6" fill="var(--redline)" />
          <circle cx="166" cy="14" r="4.5" fill="none" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="1.2" />
          <ellipse cx="90" cy="55" rx="4" ry="3" fill="none" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="1" transform="rotate(35 90 55)" />
          <ellipse cx="14" cy="96" rx="5.5" ry="3.5" fill="none" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="1.2" transform="rotate(35 14 96)" />
          <ellipse cx="166" cy="96" rx="10" ry="4.5" fill="none" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="1.5" transform="rotate(35 166 96)" />
          <text x="90" y="130" class="tilt-cap" text-anchor="middle">TILTED</text>
        </g>
        <g transform="translate(265,15)">
          <rect x="0" y="0" width="180" height="110" fill="none" stroke="var(--line-500)" stroke-width="1.5" />
          <circle cx="14" cy="14" r="2.6" fill="var(--redline)" />
          <circle cx="166" cy="14" r="2.6" fill="var(--redline)" />
          <circle cx="90" cy="55" r="2.6" fill="var(--redline)" />
          <circle cx="14" cy="96" r="2.6" fill="var(--redline)" />
          <circle cx="166" cy="96" r="2.6" fill="var(--redline)" />
          <text x="90" y="130" class="tilt-cap" text-anchor="middle">CORRECTED</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="mono tilt-caption"><span class="tilt-fignum">FIG. 01</span>STAR SHAPE ACROSS THE FRAME: TILTED VS. CORRECTED</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="tilt-figure">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 360 230" role="img" aria-label="Side-view schematic of a tilted sensor relative to the ideal focal plane">
        <!-- optical axis -->
        <line x1="15" y1="115" x2="340" y2="115" stroke="var(--line-500)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 4" />
        <text x="20" y="107" class="tilt-cap">OPTICAL AXIS</text>

        <!-- ideal focal plane, perpendicular to the axis -->
        <line x1="235" y1="40" x2="235" y2="190" stroke="var(--line-500)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
        <line x1="145" y1="26" x2="233" y2="41" stroke="var(--line-500)" stroke-width="0.75" stroke-dasharray="2 2" />
        <text x="55" y="29" class="tilt-cap">IDEAL FOCAL PLANE</text>

        <!-- tilted sensor, crossing the ideal plane on-axis -->
        <line x1="210" y1="40" x2="260" y2="190" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="1.8" />
        <line x1="222" y1="65" x2="278" y2="52" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="0.75" stroke-dasharray="2 2" />
        <text x="281" y="55" class="tilt-cap" fill="var(--redline)">TILTED SENSOR</text>

        <!-- crossing point: sharp focus -->
        <circle cx="235" cy="115" r="3.2" fill="var(--redline)" />
        <line x1="238" y1="118" x2="252" y2="140" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="0.75" stroke-dasharray="2 2" />
        <text x="238" y="152" class="tilt-cap" fill="var(--redline)">SHARP FOCUS</text>

        <!-- far end of sensor: defocused -->
        <circle cx="260" cy="190" r="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="1.3" />
        <line x1="255" y1="199" x2="235" y2="212" stroke="var(--redline)" stroke-width="0.75" stroke-dasharray="2 2" />
        <text x="150" y="217" class="tilt-cap" fill="var(--redline)">DEFOCUSED, SAME EXPOSURE</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption class="mono tilt-caption"><span class="tilt-fignum">FIG. 02</span>SENSOR TILT, SIDE VIEW (EXAGGERATED FOR CLARITY)</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    On the CarbonStar 200 that correction is done with NINA's Hocus Focus
    plugin driving a Wanderer ETA: it focuses the center and each corner
    separately in one autofocus run, then moves the adjuster's three motors
    to level the sensor. The first pass found about 45&nbsp;µm of
    top-to-bottom tilt, roughly three times the critical focus zone. The
    second pass measured every corner within ±4&nbsp;µm, and the third
    changed nothing, which shows the correction converged.
  </p>

  <div class="wf-grid3">
    <figure class="wf-fig"><img src={`${WF}tilt-pass-1.jpg`} alt="Hocus Focus pass 1 corner measurements" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Pass 1: bottom left 41 µm out</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="wf-fig"><img src={`${WF}tilt-pass-2.jpg`} alt="Hocus Focus pass 2 corner measurements" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Pass 2: all corners within ±4 µm</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="wf-fig"><img src={`${WF}tilt-pass-3.jpg`} alt="Hocus Focus pass 3 corner measurements" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Pass 3: converged</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <div class="wf-grid2">
    <figure class="wf-fig wf-plot"><img src={`${WF}tilt-field-map.jpg`} alt="Grid maps of star size and elongation across the frame before and after spacing changes" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Star size across the frame: the tilt gradient, top edge soft</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="wf-fig"><img src={`${WF}tilt-eta-corrected.jpg`} alt="WandererEmpire electronic tilt adjuster control panel after correction" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Tilt adjuster motor positions after correction</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">21:47</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Autofocus backlash</h3>
        <p>
          Several autofocus curves ended in two identical points: the
          focuser losing motion when it reverses. Setting NINA's backlash
          compensation to overshoot, so every move arrives from the same
          direction, brought both following curves to R²&nbsp;=&nbsp;1.00.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">21:54</span>
      <div>
        <h3>The guide prism shadow</h3>
        <p>
          With tilt fixed, stars along one whole edge still had comet tails,
          and bright stars in older images carried one-sided fans of rays.
          The only part of the train that sits on one side of the light path
          is the off-axis guider's pickoff prism, and it was sitting right at
          the sensor's edge. At f/4 the light cone for the edge pixels is still
          wider than the sensor at the prism's depth, so the prism had to
          clear at least 14.2&nbsp;mm from the optical center. Measured with
          calipers after retracting it: 14.5&nbsp;mm, and the guide camera
          still reached focus.
        </p>
        <div class="wf-grid2">
          <figure class="wf-fig"><img src={`${NT}ninetile-prism-shadow.jpg`} alt="NINA nine-tile view with comet-tailed stars along the top row" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Comet tails across the top row</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="wf-fig"><img src={`${NT}orion-prism-fans.jpg`} alt="Earlier Orion Nebula image with one-sided fans on bright stars" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">The same artifact in an older image</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <div class="wf-grid3">
          <figure class="wf-fig wf-crop"><img src={`${NT}prism-through-corrector.jpg`} alt="View through the coma corrector showing the guide prism at the sensor edge" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Through the corrector</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="wf-fig wf-crop"><img src={`${NT}prism-at-sensor-edge.jpg`} alt="Sensor with the guide prism holder touching its edge" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Prism holder touching the sensor edge</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="wf-fig wf-crop"><img src={`${NT}prism-retracted.jpg`} alt="Off-axis guider face after retracting the prism" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">After retracting it</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">22:53</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Verifying the fix</h3>
        <p>
          A test flat came out symmetric top to bottom to within 0.3%. On real
          data, the elongation in the top third of the frame dropped from 0.67
          to 0.39, and the top-to-bottom gap closed from 0.14 to 0.01. Stars
          now measure about 92% as wide as they are tall, which reads as round.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">23:12</span>
      <div>
        <h3>The flares that remained</h3>
        <p>
          A few of the brightest stars still flared, but these pointed away
          from the frame center in every direction. They start just past
          6.5&nbsp;mm from center on the sensor, exactly where the flat field
          starts to fall off.
        </p>
        <div class="wf-grid2">
          <figure class="wf-fig wf-plot"><img src={`${NT}flare-example.jpg`} alt="Bright star with a flare pointing away from the frame center" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">A flare pointing straight away from center</figcaption></figure>
          <figure class="wf-fig wf-plot"><img src={`${NT}flat-radial-profile.jpg`} alt="Flat-field radial profile, flat to 6.5 mm then falling off" loading="lazy" /><figcaption class="mono">Flat-field falloff starts where the flares do</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="night-time mono">23:33</span>
      <div>
        <h3>Root cause: the secondary's edge</h3>
        <p>
          Filters, dew shield, focuser, flocking, mirror mask and corrector
          were all ruled out, most decisively by the fact that the flares
          predate every mod and show up with two different correctors. Other
          CarbonStar owners have traced the same flare to an over-beveled
          secondary mirror that leaves reflective coating on its edge. The fix
          is a 3D-printed mask covering 2&ndash;3&nbsp;mm of the secondary's
          edge, which is the next change to the scope.
        </p>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h3 class="wf-subhead">What I took from it</h3>
  <ul class="night-lessons">
    <li><strong>Separate the symptoms before naming a cause.</strong> "Bad stars" was five problems, each with its own signature.</li>
    <li><strong>Change one variable at a time,</strong> and record the setting next to every frame.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure instead of eyeballing.</strong> Photos down the light path lied because of parallax; calipers and a flat didn't.</li>
    <li><strong>History is data.</strong> "The flares have been there since day one" ruled out more than any calculation.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the logs.</strong> A sequence that fails silently usually leaves an exception behind.</li>
  </ul>


  {next && (
    <a href={astroHref(next.slug)} class="astro-next">
      <span class="mono">Next</span>
      <strong>{next.title} →</strong>
      <span>{next.note}</span>
    </a>
  )}
</Layout>
